      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the prevalence of low back pain (LBP) in an Australian metropolitan, male adolescent population."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Self-reported, pretested questionnaire that included a definition of LBP and a description of the body area it affects."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Three government operated secondary schools in a growth corridor of north suburban Melbourne, Australia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "Six hundred ten males attending secondary (high) school, aged between 12 and 19 yr."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "None."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OBSERVATIONS",
          "content": "The point prevalence of LBP was found to be 16.7% and the sample prevalence 57%. The typical sufferer of adolescent LBP (ALBP) reported chronic LBP experienced up to a few days at a time, several times a month."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The high prevalence of ALBP found by studies in other populations is confirmed in Australia. There is a clear need for further study of the etiology and potential preventative factors of ALBP, and for consideration of whether nontraumatic occupational LBP results more from an aggravation of a preexisting (adolescent) condition than from an unsafe or unsuitable work environment."
        }
      ]},
